// Lesson 1.6 — Animation States: IsWalking, WalkDir, Attack
// First-introduction lesson: FULL scaffold (walkthrough level 1).
//
// `SetBool` / `SetInteger` / `SetTrigger` and `GetComponentInChildren` are NEW
// concepts here, so the animation half of PlayerController.cs is given complete
// and explained line by line. The old "Read Function-Level Examples" block
// (scuba diver / warrior / wizard / falcon) has been removed: those snippets
// taught the API in foreign domains and left the student to assemble the real
// script alone, which is the leap 1.6 kept losing students on.
//
// Prior knowledge this walkthrough deliberately re-invokes:
//   - the declare/assign split and class-body fields from 1.3
//   - `xDirection` / `yDirection`, and the derived `xVector` / `yVector`, from 1.5
//   - the child "Sprite" object from 1.2, which is where the Sprite Renderer
//     and Animator actually live — the reason GetComponent returns null here
//
// Project conventions, verified against the shipped controller:
//   Player prefab -> Player_Shovel.controller (Player_Axe exists, unassigned)
//   Parameters: IsWalking (Bool), WalkDir (Int 0 = up, 1 = sideways, 2 = down),
//   Attack (Trigger). There is no Float parameter in this controller.
//
// STRUCTURE (tightening pass): the reading-the-Animator-window walk becomes
// "In the Editor" (it is clicks in panes, and it now names each pane); the API
// table, the socratic, the walkthrough and the bug hunt are "In the Code". The
// old "Read the Docs" and "Inspect the Controller You Are Driving" labels are
// absorbed into those two, and "Test It, Then Break It" is now "Bug Hunt".
//
// CULLED FOR REPETITION AND PRE-EXPLANATION: the "In professional game engines
// scripts rarely swap images" paragraph (the table and the socratic already say
// it), the "One thing this table cannot tell you" paragraph (it restated the
// SetBool line's note and the Animator-window step's spelling warning), the
// walkthrough note that pre-answered the socratic's separation-of-concerns
// question, and the bug hunt's last two items.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderWalkthrough, renderAskBanner } from '../../lib/walkthrough';
import { renderDecompose } from '../../lib/decompose';

// The two recurring questions, applied to animation. Level 1, so every part
// is given with its `does` and its `doc`. Four parts rather than the
// walkthrough's seven steps: those seven are the line-by-line detail, and this
// block is the altitude above them — find the machine, ask a yes/no question,
// ask a which-one question, turn the picture around. The attack is deliberately
// not a part (it is a different feature, not part of "play a walking
// animation") and neither is the pair of calls from Update() (plumbing every
// part shares). Both are asked about in `turns`.
const DECOMPOSE = {
  big:
    'Play a walking animation for a character that already moves in four directions, with a ' +
    'different clip for each one and a picture that faces the way it walks.',
  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Get a handle on the Animator and the Sprite Renderer',
      does: 'Two fields, filled once, before the first frame. Both components live on the child Sprite, so only `GetComponentInChildren<T>()` finds them.',
      doc: 'the `Animator` and `flipX` rows in the quick reference.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Work out whether the player is moving at all',
      does: 'One true/false value built from the two movement fields 1.5 gave you: `xVector != 0 || yVector != 0`. The controller turns `IsWalking` into idle or walking; you never name a clip.',
      doc: 'the `SetBool("IsWalking", b)` row in the quick reference, and the Parameters tab.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Work out which direction the player is facing',
      does: 'Compare the inputs by size rather than sign — the `Mathf.Abs(float f)` row — because a hard left push is `-4` and would lose to a gentle `+1` upward. The winner sets `WalkDir`: `0` up, `1` sideways, `2` down.',
      doc: 'the row of math helpers in the quick reference.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Turn the picture around to match the direction',
      does: 'The sideways clip is drawn facing right, so the sprite is mirrored rather than redrawn: `flipX` true for a left push, false for a right one. The up and down clips face the camera.',
      doc: 'the `flipX` row in the quick reference.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
  ],
  why:
    'One part finds the machine, one asks a yes/no question, one asks a which-one question, one ' +
    'turns the picture around. Animation looks like a whole new subject; it is four decisions.',
  sizeTest:
    'Does the part name an API you could search for? "Play the walking animation" cannot be ' +
    'looked up; `SetBool("IsWalking", b)`, `SetInteger("WalkDir", 1)` and `flipX` can.',
  turns: [
    'The walkthrough fires the attack with SetTrigger(string name), and a swing is not one of ' +
      'the four parts. Why not — and what is different about an action that ends by itself?',
    'Two methods are written in the walkthrough and neither runs until something calls it. Where ' +
      'do those calls belong, and why would calling them before the input is read animate the ' +
      'wrong frame?',
  ],
};

const WALK = {
  ask: 'how do I play a walking animation?',
  file: 'Assets/Scripts/PlayerController.cs',
  label: 'In the Code',
  intro: 'The animation half of the script you already have — add it to the file from 1.5.',
  level: 1,
  steps: [
    {
      title: 'Declare the two components at class scope',
      lead: 'A script can only talk to a component it holds a reference to.',
      lines: [
        { code: '// PlayerController.cs — class body, below the speed knob and the direction fields.', what: '' },
        { code: '    Animator animator;', what: 'Will hold the Animator. **No value yet** — `Start()` fills it in.' },
        { code: '    SpriteRenderer renderer;', what: 'The same for the picture, needed for one line later: `flipX`.', why: 'Both fields start as `null`, and every `NullReferenceException` here comes from a field declared but never assigned.' },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Find them once, in Start()',
      lead: 'Add this below the fields; your file has no `Start()` yet.',
      lines: [
        { code: '// PlayerController.cs — Start() does not exist in your file yet. Add it below the fields.', what: '' },
        { code: '    void Start()', what: 'Runs once, before the first frame — the right place to look a component up.' },
        { code: '    {', what: '' },
        { code: '        renderer = GetComponentInChildren<SpriteRenderer>();', what: 'Searches the Player **and every object beneath it** for a Sprite Renderer.' },
        { code: '        animator = GetComponentInChildren<Animator>();', what: 'The same shape, for the same reason: the Animator is on the child **Sprite** too.' , why: '**The line that catches everyone.** `GetComponent<T>()` looks only at the Player, finds nothing, and hands you `null`. The `InChildren` is the whole difference, and the error it prevents appears one method later, on a line that looks fine.' },
        { code: '    }', what: '' },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Write UpdateAnimation() and set the walking flag',
      lead: 'Your own method, called once per frame. One question first: **is the player moving at all?**',
      lines: [
        { code: '// PlayerController.cs — below Update(), a method of your own.', what: '' },
        { code: '    void UpdateAnimation()', what: 'A method you write. Nothing calls it automatically — step 7 adds the call.', why: 'Animation in its own method leaves `Update()` as three ideas: read the input, move, update the visuals.' },
        { code: '    {', what: '' },
        { code: '        bool isMoving = xVector != 0 || yVector != 0;', what: 'A `bool` from 1.5\'s two fields: horizontal is not zero **or** vertical is not zero. It reads the same fields the movement line used, so it cannot disagree with where the character went.' },
        { code: '        animator.SetBool("IsWalking", isMoving);', what: 'Hands the Animator a **named parameter** and a true/false value; the controller picks the clip. You never name a clip in code.', why: 'The name must match the Animator window character for character: `"IsWalking"`, capital I, capital W.' },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Choose the direction by size',
      lead: 'Three directional states, one integer parameter: `WalkDir` `0` up, `1` sideways, `2` down. Diagonals are the interesting case.',
      lines: [
        { code: '        if (Mathf.Abs(xVector) > Mathf.Abs(yVector))', what: 'Compares the **sizes**: `Mathf.Abs(float f)` throws the sign away, so `-4` counts as `4`. If horizontal is strictly bigger, the sideways clip plays.', why: '**Strictly bigger, not `>=`.** One step of `>=` and a perfect diagonal — pressing right and up together, where the two sizes are equal — would count as sideways. With `>` the vertical branch wins the tie, which is what this game does.' },
        { code: '        {', what: '' },
        { code: '            animator.SetInteger("WalkDir", 1);', what: '`SetInteger(string name, int value)` sets an Int parameter; `1` means "walking sideways".' },
        { code: '', what: '' },
        { code: '            if (xVector < 0)', what: 'Inside the sideways branch: is the player going **left**? `xVector` goes negative on the left key.' },
        { code: '            {', what: '' },
        { code: '                renderer.flipX = true;', what: '`true` mirrors the picture left-to-right, so the character faces left.' },
        { code: '            }', what: '' },
        { code: '            else if (xVector > 0)', what: '**`else if`, not `else`.** Releasing the key makes `xVector` zero, and zero should not count as "facing right" — it should leave the sprite however it was last drawn.' },
        { code: '            {', what: '' },
        { code: '                renderer.flipX = false;', what: '`false` leaves the clip as drawn — facing right.' },
        { code: '            }', what: '' },
        { code: '        }', what: '' },
        { code: '        else if (yVector > 0)', what: 'Only reached when horizontal lost. `> 0` asks whether vertical is positive; up is `+`.', why: '**`else if` twice, not a final `else`.** Stand still and neither branch fires, so `WalkDir` keeps its last value — harmless here, because the Idle state never reads it. A final `else` would have to mean "down" and would be wrong for standing still.' },
        { code: '        {', what: '' },
        { code: '            animator.SetInteger("WalkDir", 0);', what: '`0` means walking up in this controller.' },
        { code: '        }', what: '' },
        { code: '        else if (yVector < 0)', what: 'Not sideways, not up, and moving down.' },
        { code: '        {', what: '' },
        { code: '            animator.SetInteger("WalkDir", 2);', what: '`2` means walking down — the values the transition arrows compare against.' },
        { code: '        }', what: '' },
        { code: '    }', what: '' },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Fire the attack with a trigger',
      lead: 'An attack is a one-shot, not a state the player stays in; the Trigger parameter clears itself once a transition consumes it.',
      lines: [
        { code: '// PlayerController.cs — inside Update(), after the movement line.', what: '' },
        { code: '        if (Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.E))', what: 'The `Input` class from 1.4, asking about one key. `GetKeyDown` is true on the **single frame** the key goes down.', why: '**`E` and not the mouse.** This project swings with a key, and the same key is the one the Overworld\'s Canvas already advertises — <code>Press [E] to Interact</code>. 4.6 adds two more conditions to this same `if`; nothing about the shape changes.' },
        { code: '        {', what: '' },
        { code: '            animator.SetTrigger("Attack");', what: 'Fires the trigger. It takes only a name: a trigger has no state to set.' },
        { code: '        }', what: '' },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Call the helper from Update()',
      lead: 'The method is written and nothing runs it yet: a method of your own does nothing until something calls it.',
      lines: [
        { code: '        UpdateAnimation();', what: 'Runs the animation logic every frame. The parentheses are empty because `xVector` and `yVector` are fields, already there to read.', why: '**Where it goes matters.** The flags are built from those fields, so a call placed above the input read animates last frame\'s input.' },
      ],
    },
  ],
  finalFile: `using UnityEngine;

public class PlayerController : MonoBehaviour
{
    float speed = 4;

    float xDirection;
    float yDirection;
    float xVector;
    float yVector;

    Animator animator;
    SpriteRenderer renderer;

    void Start()
    {
        renderer = GetComponentInChildren<SpriteRenderer>();
        animator = GetComponentInChildren<Animator>();
    }

    void Update()
    {
        xDirection = Input.GetAxisRaw("Horizontal");
        yDirection = Input.GetAxisRaw("Vertical");

        xVector = xDirection * speed;
        yVector = yDirection * speed;

        transform.Translate(new Vector2(xVector, yVector) * Time.deltaTime);

        if (Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.E))
        {
            animator.SetTrigger("Attack");
        }

        UpdateAnimation();
    }

    void UpdateAnimation()
    {
        bool isMoving = xVector != 0 || yVector != 0;
        animator.SetBool("IsWalking", isMoving);

        if (Mathf.Abs(xVector) > Mathf.Abs(yVector))
        {
            animator.SetInteger("WalkDir", 1);

            if (xVector < 0)
            {
                renderer.flipX = true;
            }
            else if (xVector > 0)
            {
                renderer.flipX = false;
            }
        }
        else if (yVector > 0)
        {
            animator.SetInteger("WalkDir", 0);
        }
        else if (yVector < 0)
        {
            animator.SetInteger("WalkDir", 2);
        }
    }
}`,
  finalNote:
    'Compare it against your own file rather than pasting it in: both components assigned in `Start()` with `GetComponentInChildren<T>()`, `UpdateAnimation()` actually called from `Update()`, the three parameter names spelled as they are in the Animator window, and `flipX` true on the left and false on the right.',
};

const body = `<h1>1.6 — Animation States: IsWalking, WalkDir, Attack</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">Your character moves, but it does not <em>look</em> like it: it slides around in the same idle pose whichever way you push. A script never plays an animation itself — it <em>asks</em> the Animator by setting parameters, and the Animator picks the clip.</p>

${renderAskBanner('in unity how to set an animator parameter from code, how to use setbool and setinteger on an animator, how to change the direction of a walking animation', 1)}

${renderDecompose('1.6', DECOMPOSE, 1)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header"><span class="activity-label">In the Editor</span><span class="activity-time">~10 min</span></div>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Hierarchy →</strong> select the child <strong>Sprite</strong> under Player — from <a href="1.2.html">1.2</a>, that is where the Animator lives, not on the Player.</li>
        <li><strong>Window → Animation → Animator.</strong> Click the <strong>Parameters</strong> tab, top-left. Three parameters are configured: <code>IsWalking</code> (Bool), <code>WalkDir</code> (Int) and <code>Attack</code> (Trigger). Write down their exact spellings, capitals included.</li>
        <li><strong>Animator →</strong> click the arrows between the up, side and down states and read their conditions in the <strong>Inspector</strong>: they compare <code>WalkDir</code> to <code>0</code>, <code>1</code> and <code>2</code>. Which number means which direction?</li>
        <li><strong>Animator →</strong> click the arrow into any of the <code>Shovel_Swing</code> states. What two conditions must <em>both</em> be true before the attack plays?</li>
        <li><strong>Inspector →</strong> with the Sprite selected, read the Animator component: which controller asset is assigned, and which kind of parameter — Bool, Int, Float or Trigger — is <em>missing</em> from it? Then <strong>Game view →</strong> press <strong>Play</strong> and press <strong>E</strong> to swing.</li>
    </ol>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header"><span class="activity-label">In the Code</span><span class="activity-time">~20 min</span></div>
    <p>The calls that do the talking between your script and the controller:</p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Unity API</th><th>Signature</th><th>Behaviour</th></tr>
        <tr><td><code>animator.SetBool()</code></td><td><code>void SetBool(string name, bool value)</code></td><td>Sets a boolean parameter, for <em>persistent</em> states — idle versus walking, where true and false are both meaningful.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>animator.SetInteger()</code></td><td><code>void SetInteger(string name, int value)</code></td><td>Sets a whole-number parameter, for <em>discrete</em> choices such as a direction (0, 1, 2), where each value is a different clip.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>animator.SetFloat()</code></td><td><code>void SetFloat(string name, float value)</code></td><td>Sets a decimal parameter, for smooth values such as speed. The fourth member of the family — <strong>this controller has no Float parameter</strong>, so you will not call it today.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>animator.SetTrigger()</code></td><td><code>void SetTrigger(string name)</code></td><td>Fires a momentary trigger, which resets itself once a transition consumes it, for <em>one-shot</em> actions such as attacks. No value is passed.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>renderer.flipX</code></td><td><code>bool</code> (read/write)</td><td>Mirrors the sprite horizontally, letting one sideways walk clip serve both left and right.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Mathf.Abs()</code></td><td><code>float Mathf.Abs(float f)</code></td><td>Returns a number's absolute value, stripping the sign — for comparing size regardless of direction.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>GetComponentInChildren&lt;T&gt;()</code></td><td><code>T GetComponentInChildren&lt;T&gt;()</code></td><td>Finds a component on this GameObject <em>or on any of its children</em> — where the Sprite Renderer and the Animator turned out to live.</td></tr>
    </table>
    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Separation of Concerns</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Why does your script set abstract parameters like <code>"IsWalking"</code> rather than telling the engine "play frame 4 of the walk sprite sheet"?</li>
            <li>If an artist replaced a 4-frame walk animation with an 8-frame one, how much C# would change? What does your answer say about this split?</li>
            <li>What is the difference between <code>animator.SetBool(string name, bool value)</code> and <code>animator.SetTrigger(string name)</code>? Which resets itself, and why does that matter for an attack?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
${renderWalkthrough(WALK)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header"><span class="activity-label">Bug Hunt</span><span class="activity-time">~25 min</span></div>
    <p>Walk in all four directions and press <strong>E</strong> to swing. Once that works, break one piece at a time.</p>
    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Diagnosing Animation Faults</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Misspell <code>"IsWalking"</code> as <code>"isWalking"</code> (lowercase i). What does the Console report? Why is a misspelled parameter a warning rather than a crash?</li>
            <li><strong>Delete the <code>UpdateAnimation();</code> call</strong> from <code>Update()</code> and press Play. No errors appear and the character still moves — so what does it <em>look</em> like, and what does that prove?</li>
            <li>Comment out both <code>renderer.flipX</code> lines. Walk left: which way does the sprite face, and can any input make it face the other way?</li>
            <li>Change <code>GetComponentInChildren&lt;Animator&gt;()</code> to <code>GetComponent&lt;Animator&gt;()</code>. What error appears, and which line does Unity point at — the one in <code>Start()</code> or the one in <code>UpdateAnimation()</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

